message.content:
Below is a minimal Java example that shows how you would use the **`XContentBuilder_map`** helper to serialize a `HashMap` called `userProfile`.  
The helper takes three arguments:

| Argument | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `values` | The variable name (as a string) that holds the map you want to serialize (`"userProfile"`). |
| `ensureNoSelfReferences` | `"true"` – guarantees that the map does not contain a reference to itself (prevents stack‑overflow). |
| `writeStartAndEndHeaders` | `"true"` – writes the `{}` delimiters automatically so you don’t have to call `startObject()` / `endObject()` yourself. |

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

// 1. Build the map you want to serialize
Map<String, Object> userProfile = new HashMap<>();
userProfile.put("name",  "John Doe");
userProfile.put("age",   30);
userProfile.put("email", "john.doe@example.com");

// 2. Create an XContentBuilder (JSON format here)
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();

// 3. Call the helper – note that the arguments are strings
functions.XContentBuilder_map({
    values: "userProfile",
    ensureNoSelfReferences: "true",
    writeStartAndEndHeaders: "true"
});

// 4. Convert to a JSON string (or write to a stream, etc.)
String json = builder.string();
System.out.println(json);   // {"name":"John Doe","age":30,"email":"john.doe@example.com"}
```

### What happens under the hood?

* Because `writeStartAndEndHeaders` is set to `"true"`, the helper automatically emits the opening `{` and closing `}` around the map’s contents.
* Setting `ensureNoSelfReferences` to `"true"` makes the serializer check that none of the map’s values refer back to the map itself, preventing infinite recursion.

Feel free to replace the `jsonBuilder()` with another content type (e.g., `yamlBuilder()`) if you need a different serialization format.

